{"schema_version":"1.0.0","metadata":{"trial_id":"202610080659-townhouse-row","prompting_method_id":"concept-build-free.v0","model_id":"claude-opus-5-5","seed":11,"server_state_id":"concept-free.v1"},"style":{"name":"canal-row merchant townhouses (Dutch/Hanseatic quay)","rationale":"Second pass, judged from the street. 1) The first build read as a dark colonnade: spruce posts ran full height across every bay and drowned the facades. Now posts stand only at the corners of A and C, 1 proud on a corbel course, with a stripped-spruce floor beam at y9 and a top plate at y12, so the frame boxes the oak infill the way the concept does. B stays flush brick. 2) The roofline was unresolved. A now has a true front-facing gable with the ridge along z: spruce stair courses, an oak-stair verge at z=-2, a west eave overhang, a gable window and a hoist beam with a chain at the apex. B's stepped Dutch gable is rebuilt cleanly in 6/4/2 steps with brick_slab caps, a terracotta inner panel and a recessed attic window. Its brick roof follows the same stepped profile behind it, so the gable and roof read as one form and not a loose cutout. C is a clean flat roof: a stone-brick cornice at y13 on the shared datum, a stone_brick_wall parapet with explicit connection states, a grass garden and a 3x3 spruce pergola. 3) Depth: every front wall is 2 thick, so doors and windows sit 1 in and the jambs read as wall thickness. A corbel course of upside-down stone-brick stairs plus the y5 slab string course runs the full row and ties the three houses together. B gets a corbelled brick cornice at y12 so its eaves line matches A's top plate and C's cornice. 4) Ground floor invites you in: recessed doors with spruce trapdoor hoods, slab doorsteps, shop signs beside the A, B and C doors, and a barrel and crate stack by B. 5) Side faces of A (west) and C (east) are finished with a stone base, framed oak upper floors, corner posts, beams and windows. The back is plain, with one window per floor. 6) Street: cobble sidewalk, a quay face with stone_brick_wall coping at z=-3, and three lanterns on dark-oak posts (A's end, the B|C line, C's corner), matching the concept. Vanilla 1.20.1 has no dyed doors, so the blue/green/red trio maps to warped (blue-teal), jungle (stands in for green) and mangrove (red). Window-box pots echo the doors: blue orchid on A, fern on B, red tulip on C. Palette hierarchy kept: stone dominant, oak/brick supporting, spruce framing, terracotta and doors as accents."},"palette":{"manifest":["minecraft:stone_bricks","minecraft:cobblestone","minecraft:stone_brick_wall","minecraft:stone_brick_slab","minecraft:stone_brick_stairs","minecraft:oak_planks","minecraft:bricks","minecraft:brick_slab","minecraft:brick_stairs","minecraft:terracotta","minecraft:spruce_log","minecraft:stripped_spruce_log","minecraft:spruce_planks","minecraft:spruce_stairs","minecraft:spruce_slab","minecraft:oak_stairs","minecraft:oak_slab","minecraft:spruce_fence","minecraft:spruce_trapdoor","minecraft:spruce_wall_sign","minecraft:glass_pane","minecraft:warped_door","minecraft:jungle_door","minecraft:mangrove_door","minecraft:dark_oak_fence","minecraft:lantern","minecraft:chain","minecraft:barrel","minecraft:crafting_table","minecraft:grass_block","minecraft:poppy","minecraft:oxeye_daisy","minecraft:flowering_azalea","minecraft:potted_blue_orchid","minecraft:potted_fern","minecraft:potted_red_tulip"]},"placements":[{"op":"fill","from":[-1,-2,-3],"to":[20,0,-3],"block":"minecraft:stone_bricks"},{"op":"line","from":[-1,1,-3],"to":[20,1,-3],"block":"minecraft:stone_brick_wall","state":{"east":"low","west":"low","up":"false"}},{"op":"fill","from":[-1,0,-2],"to":[20,0,-1],"block":"minecraft:cobblestone"},{"op":"fill","from":[-1,0,0],"to":[-1,0,10],"block":"minecraft:cobblestone"},{"op":"fill","from":[20,0,0],"to":[20,0,10],"block":"minecraft:cobblestone"},{"op":"fill","from":[0,0,0],"to":[19,0,9],"block":"minecraft:stone_bricks"},{"op":"fill","from":[0,1,1],"to":[19,4,1],"block":"mi